# Forbidden Commands

The agent must not run destructive or system-breaking commands without explicit user approval.

A command is forbidden if it can delete important data, destroy infrastructure, expose secrets, overwrite history, break the local machine, damage containers, modify production, or make changes that are difficult to reverse.

## Core Rule

Before running any dangerous command, the agent must stop and ask for explicit approval.

The agent must explain:

1. The exact command it wants to run.
2. Why it is risky.
3. What data, files, services, containers, databases, or infrastructure it may affect.
4. The safer alternative, if one exists.

The agent must not run the command until the user clearly approves it.

## System-Breaking File Commands

Never run these commands without explicit approval:

```bash
rm -rf /
rm -rf /*
rm -rf ./*
rm -rf .[^.]*
rm -rf ~
rm -rf ~/.
rm -rf $HOME
rm -rf "$HOME"
rm -rf /home
rm -rf /usr
rm -rf /etc
rm -rf /var
rm -rf /opt
rm -rf /tmp/*
find / -delete
find . -delete
find . -type f -delete
truncate -s 0 *
shred -u *
wipe *